<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Biberach an der Riss</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Biberach an der Riß</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Biberach an der <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/riss">Riss</a> is a town in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-swabia">Upper Swabia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baden-wurttemberg">Baden-Württemberg</a>, on the river Riss. A market town under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hohenstaufen">Hohenstaufen</a>, it was a free imperial city from the late 13th century until 1803 and prospered from fustian weaving. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirty-tyrants">the Thirty</a> Years' War it was governed under strict parity between Catholics and Lutherans. The writer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christoph-martin-wieland">Christoph Martin Wieland</a> was born nearby in 1733.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/biberach-an-der-riss.jpg" alt="Biberach an der Riss" width="320" height="432" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bene16 · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:BiberachSt.Martin15-8.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
